course: biology
lesson: 321
title: Big snakes: pythons, boas and anacondas, and how big they really get
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Pythons, boas and anacondas are the giants of the snake world. They have no venom; they hunt by grabbing prey and wrapping it in their coils, which is why they are called constrictors.
explain: The longest snake is the reticulated python of Southeast Asia, and the heaviest is the green anaconda of South America. The biggest really measured were about 7 metres, as long as two small cars parked nose to tail.
explain: Stories always make big snakes bigger. Old posters show 15-metre monsters swallowing canoes, but a reward for a living snake over about 9 metres was reportedly never claimed, and a snake held close to a camera looks far bigger than it is.
explain: Their bodies are full of surprises: a few hundred backbones, two tiny spurs left over from the legs of their lizard ancestors, jaws that stretch around an egg wider than the head, and a heart that grows after a big meal.
explain: Think of a python in a museum skeleton: it looks like a long zip with a rib on each side of every tooth.
fact: The reticulated python of Southeast Asia is the longest snake; the longest reliably measured ones were about 7 metres, though stories claim far more.
fact: The green anaconda of South America is the heaviest snake; a big female can weigh well over 100 kilograms, and females are much bigger than males.
fact: Anacondas live mostly in swamps and slow rivers, where the water helps hold up their heavy bodies.
fact: No snake anywhere near 15 metres long has ever been measured; a reward offered over a hundred years ago for a living snake longer than about 9 metres was reportedly never claimed.
fact: Constrictors coil around prey and tighten each time it breathes out; scientists found the squeeze mainly stops the blood flowing, so the prey faints within moments rather than being crushed.
fact: A big python has a few hundred backbones, often around 300 or more, and nearly every one carries a pair of ribs; you have 33 backbones.
fact: Boas and pythons have two small spurs near the tail, the last tiny bits of back legs from the lizards with legs that snakes evolved from.
fact: The two halves of a snake's lower jaw are joined by stretchy tissue and the jaw bones are loosely joined to the skull; snakes do not «unhinge» their jaws, and they swallow prey whole, usually head first.
fact: A big python can live for months, sometimes over a year, after one large meal, because a cold-blooded animal burns very little energy; its heart and gut grow bigger for a few days to digest the meal.
fact: Burmese pythons let go or escaped from people's homes now live wild in the Everglades of Florida, where they have eaten huge numbers of small mammals and birds; the biggest caught there was reported at about 5.5 metres.
fact: Female pythons coil around their eggs to guard them, and a Burmese python mother can twitch her muscles to keep them several degrees warmer than the air; boas and anacondas give birth to live babies.
fact: Snakes cannot hypnotise anyone; they have no eyelids, only a clear scale over each eye, so they stare without blinking.
exercise: Which snake is the longest in the world? | The reticulated python | The green anaconda | The king cobra | The black mamba
exercise: About how long were the longest reticulated pythons ever reliably measured? | About 7 metres | About 15 metres | Only about 2 metres | About 30 metres
exercise: Which snake is the heaviest? | The green anaconda | The reticulated python | The common grass snake | The diamondback rattlesnake
exercise: In anacondas, which are bigger? | Females, sometimes several times heavier than males | Males, which are always about twice the size of the females | Both are always exactly the same size | Babies, which shrink as they grow old
exercise: Why does life in swamps and rivers suit a heavy anaconda? | The water helps hold up its heavy body | The water makes it lighter for ever | Anacondas cannot breathe air at all | Rivers have no other animals in them
exercise: What did a reward for a living snake over about 9 metres show? | It was reportedly never claimed, so such giants are not found | Many hunters claimed it every year with snakes of 15 metres or more | Snakes of 15 metres were common then | The reward was paid for a canoe-eating snake
exercise: How does a constrictor's squeeze make its prey faint so quickly? | It mainly stops the blood flowing | It crushes every single bone in the body at once | It squeezes out all the stomach | It injects a strong venom
exercise: When does a python tighten its coils a little more? | Each time the prey breathes out | Only when the prey makes a sound | Once every hour, exactly by the clock | Never, it squeezes just once and waits
exercise: About how many backbones does a big python have? | A few hundred, often around 300 or more | About 33, exactly the same number as you have | About 12, one for each metre | None, snakes are all muscle
exercise: What are the two tiny spurs near a boa's tail? | The last small bits of back legs | Baby teeth that fell out and got stuck | Little stings full of strong venom | Fins left over from living in the sea
exercise: Do snakes «unhinge» their jaws to swallow big eggs? | No, the joints are loose and stretchy but nothing comes apart | Yes, the whole lower jaw falls off for the meal and then grows back | Yes, they pull the whole skull open | No, snakes chew eggs into small bits
exercise: Why can a python go without food for months after a big meal? | Being cold-blooded it burns very little energy | It stores spare food in a pouch on its belly like a kangaroo | It eats grass between meals in secret | It sleeps for exactly one year
exercise: Why do hunters catch Burmese pythons in the Florida Everglades? | Released or escaped pets live wild there and eat many animals | They are a rare kind that needs saving, so hunters take them to safe zoos | They escaped from a ship of anacondas | They swim there from Africa every year
exercise: How does a Burmese python mother keep her eggs warm? | She coils round them and twitches her muscles | She lays them on a warm road | She covers them with feathers | She carries them in her mouth all day
exercise: Which of these big snakes give birth to live babies? | Boas and anacondas | Reticulated and Burmese pythons | All pythons and no boas | None, all snakes lay eggs
exercise: Why does a snake stare without blinking? | It has no eyelids, only a clear scale over each eye | It blinks far too fast for people to ever see it happen | It is trying to hypnotise you | Its eyelids are glued shut by venom
test: «mate there's a 15 metre anaconda that ate a whole canoe, saw the poster» What is the best reply? | No snake anywhere near that long has ever been measured; posters exaggerate | It is real, and 15 metres is only a small one; the big ones are twice as long | True, they eat canoes every summer | True, but only in the Everglades
test: Count them: a museum has 3 python skeletons. How many legs and how many little leg spurs? | No legs, but 6 spurs | 12 legs and no spurs | 6 legs and 6 spurs | No legs and no spurs
test: Count them: a python has 300 backbones and almost every one has two ribs. About how many ribs is that? | About 600 | Only about 300 | Only about 150 | Only about 33
test: Count them: 4 anacondas lie in a river. How many outer ears can you see? | None, snakes have no outer ears | 8, two on each head | 4, one each | 16, four each
test: In The Jungle Book Kaa hypnotises Mowgli with spinning eyes. What is the real truth? | Real snakes cannot hypnotise; they just stare because they have no eyelids | Pythons really can hypnotise children by staring, but only for a few minutes | Only old pythons can do it | Snakes hypnotise by humming a song
test: In a cartoon a snake chews its dinner into small bits with big flat teeth. What would a real python do? | Swallow it whole, usually head first | Chew it slowly for an hour, like a cow | Cut it into pieces with its spurs first | Suck it up like a drink through a straw
test: «ya can't let him go in the woods?? the python's too big for the tank» What is right? | No, a released python can survive and harm wild animals, as in Florida | Yes, a pet python dies within a day outside, so it can do no harm to anything | Yes, snakes always find their way home | Yes, wild snakes will look after it
test: What would happen if a python squeezed a rat but the rat's heart was still beating? | The snake would feel it through its coils and keep squeezing | The snake would let go at once and slide away to find an easier meal | The snake would start to chew | The snake would fall asleep at once
test: Someone holds a small snake right in front of the camera and stands back. Why does the snake look huge in the photo? | Things nearer the camera look bigger than they are | The camera adds metres to snakes | The snake puffs up for photos | Snakes really grow when they are scared
test: A python has just eaten a deer and lies still for days. What is happening inside? | Its heart and gut have grown bigger to digest the meal | It is slowly dying, because the meal was far too big for it | It is shedding all its bones | It is getting ready to lay the deer like an egg
test: «why's a snake shivering, it's cold-blooded innit» What is the best answer? | A python mother twitches her muscles to warm her eggs | Snakes shiver only when they are ill | Snakes never move their muscles at all | It is scared of the eggs
exam: Identify the species of snake that is the longest on the basis of reliable measurements. | The reticulated python, reliably measured at approximately seven metres | The green anaconda, reliably measured at approximately fifteen metres | The king cobra, reliably measured at approximately ten metres | The black mamba, reliably measured at approximately nine metres
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the method by which constrictors subdue their prey. | The coils tighten upon each exhalation and principally arrest the circulation of the blood | The coils crush every bone of the prey at the first contraction, death following from the fractures alone | The coils deliver venom through the scales of the snake | The coils are employed solely for the transport of prey
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the small spurs found near the tail of boas and pythons? | They are vestiges of the hind limbs of the limbed lizards from which snakes evolved | They are newly evolving legs that will lengthen gradually in the course of future generations | They are organs for the delivery of venom | They are teeth that have migrated along the body
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a python may survive for many months without food following a large meal. | As a cold-blooded animal it expends very little energy | It obtains nourishment from vegetation during this period | It stores the prey alive within its body | It ceases all bodily activity until spring
